(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-4-appendicular-musculature-quiz-version-3"]={"title":"The Appendicular Musculature — Anatomy Exam 4 — Set 3","category":"Anatomy","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which muscle protracts the scapula and holds it against the thoracic wall, and is nicknamed the 'boxer's muscle'?","choices":["The rhomboid major","The serratus anterior","The levator scapulae","The pectoralis minor"],"answer":1,"exp":"The serratus anterior protracts the scapula and holds its medial border against the rib cage; weakness produces a winged scapula. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Injury to the long thoracic nerve supplying the serratus anterior produces which characteristic sign?","choices":["A dropped wrist","A clawed hand","A winged scapula","A flat-footed gait"],"answer":2,"exp":"Loss of serratus anterior function from long thoracic nerve injury causes the medial scapular border to lift away from the chest, a winged scapula. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which large superficial back muscle elevates, retracts, and depresses the scapula depending on the fibers used?","choices":["The serratus anterior","The subclavius","The trapezius","The teres major"],"answer":2,"exp":"The trapezius is a broad superficial muscle whose upper, middle, and lower fibers elevate, retract, and depress the scapula respectively. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which two muscles primarily retract (adduct) the scapula toward the vertebral column?","choices":["The serratus anterior and pectoralis minor","The deltoid and supraspinatus","The biceps and brachialis","The rhomboid major and rhomboid minor"],"answer":3,"exp":"The rhomboid major and minor run from the vertebrae to the medial scapular border and retract the scapula toward the spine. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which muscle is the prime mover for abduction of the arm at the shoulder past the first several degrees?","choices":["The deltoid muscle","The supraspinatus","The pectoralis major","The latissimus dorsi"],"answer":0,"exp":"The deltoid, especially its middle fibers, is the prime mover for abduction of the arm at the glenohumeral joint. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which rotator cuff muscle initiates the first roughly fifteen degrees of arm abduction?","choices":["The subscapularis","The supraspinatus","The teres major","The infraspinatus"],"answer":1,"exp":"The supraspinatus initiates abduction of the arm for the first roughly fifteen degrees before the deltoid takes over. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which broad muscle of the back extends, adducts, and medially rotates the arm, and is called the 'swimmer's muscle'?","choices":["The trapezius muscle","The latissimus dorsi","The rhomboid major","The levator scapulae"],"answer":1,"exp":"The latissimus dorsi extends, adducts, and medially rotates the humerus and is nicknamed the swimmer's muscle. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"The four rotator cuff muscles are the supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, and which fourth muscle?","choices":["The subscapularis","The teres major","The deltoid muscle","The coracobrachialis"],"answer":0,"exp":"The rotator cuff comprises the supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, and subscapularis, which stabilize the shoulder joint. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which rotator cuff muscle is the main medial rotator of the arm, arising from the anterior scapula?","choices":["The infraspinatus","The teres minor","The supraspinatus","The subscapularis"],"answer":3,"exp":"The subscapularis arises from the subscapular fossa on the anterior scapula and is the principal medial rotator of the arm. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which muscle is the prime mover of elbow flexion and also supinates the forearm?","choices":["The triceps brachii","The pronator teres","The biceps brachii","The anconeus"],"answer":2,"exp":"The biceps brachii flexes the elbow and is a powerful supinator of the forearm, especially against resistance. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which muscle is the prime mover of elbow extension?","choices":["The triceps brachii","The biceps brachii","The brachialis","The brachioradialis"],"answer":0,"exp":"The triceps brachii, on the posterior arm, is the prime mover of elbow extension. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which deep anterior arm muscle lies beneath the biceps and is considered the strongest pure flexor of the elbow?","choices":["The supinator muscle","The brachialis","The pronator quadratus","The triceps brachii"],"answer":1,"exp":"The brachialis lies deep to the biceps and is the strongest pure flexor of the elbow, acting regardless of forearm position. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Muscles of the anterior compartment of the forearm generally perform which action on the wrist and fingers?","choices":["Extension of the wrist and fingers","Abduction of the whole arm","Flexion of the wrist and fingers","Rotation of the scapula"],"answer":2,"exp":"The anterior forearm compartment contains the flexor muscles, which flex the wrist and fingers. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"The extrinsic muscles that move the hand and fingers have their bellies located where?","choices":["In the palm of the hand","In the forearm","In the upper arm","In the shoulder"],"answer":1,"exp":"Extrinsic hand muscles have their muscle bellies in the forearm and send long tendons to the hand and fingers. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"The intrinsic muscles forming the fleshy mass at the base of the thumb are collectively called what?","choices":["The thenar muscles","The hypothenar muscles","The lumbrical muscles","The interosseous muscles"],"answer":0,"exp":"The thenar muscles form the thenar eminence, the fleshy mass at the base of the thumb, and move the thumb. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"The hypothenar eminence is the fleshy mass at the base of which digit?","choices":["The thumb","The index finger","The middle finger","The little finger"],"answer":3,"exp":"The hypothenar eminence is the fleshy mass at the base of the little finger, formed by the hypothenar muscles. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which muscle group, crossing the front of the hip, is the primary flexor of the thigh at the hip?","choices":["The gluteus maximus","The hamstring group","The gastrocnemius","The iliopsoas"],"answer":3,"exp":"The iliopsoas, formed by the iliacus and psoas major, is the primary flexor of the thigh at the hip joint. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which muscle is the largest of the gluteal group and the main extensor of the thigh, used in climbing stairs?","choices":["The gluteus medius","The gluteus minimus","The piriformis muscle","The gluteus maximus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The gluteus maximus is the largest gluteal muscle and the main extensor of the thigh, powerful in climbing and rising from sitting. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Weakness of the gluteus medius and minimus, which normally abduct the thigh, produces which sign when walking?","choices":["A winged scapula on the back","A wrist-drop deformity","A clawed-hand deformity","A Trendelenburg (hip-drop) sign"],"answer":3,"exp":"Weak gluteus medius and minimus fail to stabilize the pelvis, so the opposite hip drops during walking, the Trendelenburg sign. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"The muscles of the medial thigh compartment, such as the adductor longus and gracilis, primarily perform which action?","choices":["Adduction of the thigh","Abduction of the thigh","Dorsiflexion of the foot","Extension of the elbow"],"answer":0,"exp":"The medial compartment muscles, including the adductor longus, brevis, magnus, and gracilis, primarily adduct the thigh. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which four-part anterior thigh muscle group is the prime extensor of the knee?","choices":["The hamstring group","The quadriceps femoris","The gluteal group","The adductor group"],"answer":1,"exp":"The quadriceps femoris, comprising the rectus femoris and three vasti, is the prime extensor of the knee. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which of the four quadriceps muscles also flexes the thigh at the hip because it crosses two joints?","choices":["The vastus lateralis","The vastus medialis","The rectus femoris","The vastus intermedius"],"answer":2,"exp":"The rectus femoris crosses both the hip and knee, so besides extending the knee it also flexes the thigh at the hip. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"The posterior thigh muscles known as the hamstrings extend the hip and perform which action at the knee?","choices":["Extension of the knee","Abduction of the hip","Flexion of the knee","Dorsiflexion of the ankle"],"answer":2,"exp":"The hamstrings extend the thigh at the hip and flex the leg at the knee. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Which three muscles make up the hamstring group of the posterior thigh?","choices":["Rectus femoris and the three vasti","Gluteus maximus, medius, minimus","Biceps femoris, semitendinosus, semimembranosus","Adductor longus, brevis, magnus"],"answer":2,"exp":"The hamstrings are the biceps femoris, semitendinosus, and semimembranosus of the posterior thigh. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Which posterior leg muscles insert via the calcaneal (Achilles) tendon to plantarflex the foot?","choices":["The gastrocnemius and soleus","The tibialis anterior and toe extensors","The fibularis longus and brevis","The quadriceps femoris muscles"],"answer":0,"exp":"The gastrocnemius and soleus join to form the calcaneal (Achilles) tendon and plantarflex the foot at the ankle. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which anterior leg muscle is the prime dorsiflexor of the foot at the ankle?","choices":["The gastrocnemius muscle","The tibialis anterior","The soleus muscle","The fibularis longus"],"answer":1,"exp":"The tibialis anterior in the anterior leg compartment is the prime dorsiflexor of the foot and also inverts it. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"The muscles that move the lower limb are commonly organized by their location into what?","choices":["Rows within the tarsal bones","Compartments separated by fascia","Rings around each joint","Sheets inside the marrow cavity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Lower-limb muscles are grouped into compartments bounded by fascia, with each compartment sharing broad actions and a nerve supply. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which anterior chest muscle adducts, medially rotates, and flexes the arm at the shoulder?","choices":["The pectoralis major","The trapezius muscle","The rhomboid minor","The infraspinatus"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pectoralis major is the large anterior chest muscle that adducts, medially rotates, and flexes the arm at the shoulder. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which muscle assists the latissimus dorsi in adducting and medially rotating the arm and runs from the scapula to the humerus?","choices":["The teres major","The teres minor","The supraspinatus","The deltoid muscle"],"answer":0,"exp":"The teres major runs from the inferior scapula to the humerus and assists the latissimus dorsi in adducting and medially rotating the arm. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which muscle forms the lateral bulge of the forearm and flexes the elbow with the forearm in a thumb-up position?","choices":["The pronator teres","The triceps brachii","The supinator muscle","The brachioradialis"],"answer":3,"exp":"The brachioradialis forms the lateral forearm bulge and flexes the elbow most effectively with the forearm midway between pronation and supination. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 29"}]};
